# ───────────────────────────────────────────
# PhD Atlas — Production Docker Compose config
# ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to .env, restrict it to the operator, and fill both required
# values before the first public boot. Use only the repository's compose.yaml:
# it pins the durable database, generated keys, uploads, and restart fuse to the
# named /app/storage volume. Never commit the populated .env file.

# Your public HTTPS domain (required)
DOMAIN=https://phd.example.com

# Required first-run operator secret (32–512 UTF-8 bytes). Generate it with a
# password manager or `openssl rand -base64 48`, enter the same value once on
# the setup screen, then rotate/remove it after the administrator exists.
PHD_ATLAS_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN=

# Auto-derived from DOMAIN unless overridden:
# BASE_URL=https://phd.example.com
# CORS_ORIGIN=https://phd.example.com
# ALLOWED_HOSTS=phd.example.com

# ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional overrides
# ───────────────────────────────────────────

# Server port (default: 4317)
# PORT=4317

# Native deployment only: custom data directory for database, uploads, backups,
# and secrets (default: ./storage). Compose intentionally overrides this with
# /app/storage so .env cannot detach durable state from its named volume.
# PHD_ATLAS_STORAGE_ROOT=/data/phd-atlas

# Docker host port mapping (compose only)
# APP_PORT=4317

# ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Capacity and low-resource controls
# ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Defaults below are designed to degrade with structured busy responses instead
# of exhausting a roughly 512 MB server. Keep them unchanged unless monitoring
# shows sustained headroom; reducing them trades peak throughput for lower RAM.

# Campus/company NATs may carry many authenticated users, while each account
# keeps its own tighter ceiling. Login has a separate anomaly ceiling because
# its token does not exist yet.
# AUTHENTICATED_NETWORK_MAX_PER_MINUTE=12000
# AUTHENTICATED_USER_MAX_PER_MINUTE=600
# AUTH_LOGIN_NETWORK_MAX_PER_MINUTE=1200

# Strict process-local LRU bound across anonymous/IP/token/email rate-limit
# identities. A lower value saves memory but lets identity churn evict sooner.
# RATE_LIMIT_BUCKET_MAX_ENTRIES=20000

# Shared RSS pressure guard. When unset, Node's container/job memory limit is
# used; hosts without a reported limit use the standalone 1024 MiB profile.
# Soft pressure pauses heavy writes/AI/uploads/background work, while hard
# pressure keeps only the exact liveness endpoint available until RSS recovers.
# RUNTIME_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES=1073741824
# RUNTIME_MEMORY_SOFT_RATIO=0.75
# RUNTIME_MEMORY_HARD_RATIO=0.875
# RUNTIME_MEMORY_HYSTERESIS_RATIO=0.05
# RUNTIME_MEMORY_RECOVERY_SAMPLES=2
# RUNTIME_MEMORY_RETRY_AFTER_MS=1000

# libuv worker threads for Argon2, fs, and zlib. start-server.mjs auto-spawns
# with 8–32 threads when this is unset; pinned deployments should keep at
# least 8 and leave at least two threads for non-password work.
# UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE=8

# Explicitly enabled stateless password workers. 0 (the default) keeps the
# existing single process and is the supported deployment shape. When enabled,
# the primary still owns SQLite and all DB routes; workers only receive pure
# Argon2 verify/hash work over cluster IPC. Each worker gets its own libuv
# pool, so raise the OS/cgroup memory limit by roughly the worker count.
#
# Setting this is what makes the concurrency qualification pass. Measured with
# npm run qa:concurrency at 100 users, two runs per configuration:
#
#   PHD_ATLAS_CLUSTER_WORKERS=0   login p95 5327 / 3060ms   mixed p95 3049ms   FAIL
#   PHD_ATLAS_CLUSTER_WORKERS=4   login p95 2698 / 2371ms   mixed p95 2386ms   PASS
#
# Login is 100 simultaneous Argon2id hashes and is CPU-bound by construction,
# which is why spreading it across processes is the thing that moves it; both
# configurations report correctnessPassed with zero write conflicts. Four
# workers is what was measured -- size it to the host and remember each one
# adds a libuv pool and its own resident memory.
# PHD_ATLAS_CLUSTER_WORKERS=0
# PHD_ATLAS_CLUSTER_WORKER_THREADPOOL_SIZE=8

# Capacity sizing by expected concurrent users:
#   ≤20 users: 512 MiB RUNTIME_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES
#   ≤100 users: 1–2 GiB RUNTIME_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES
# Keep the OS/cgroup memory limit above the configured application budget.

# External database adapters and whole-file SQLite encryption currently persist
# a complete database image per durable write. Their configured database-image
# cap defaults to 64 MiB and is automatically lowered when its worst-case
# working set would exceed 70% of RUNTIME_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES. Plain local
# SQLite remains incremental and is not subject to this snapshot cap.
# PHD_ATLAS_SNAPSHOT_MAX_BYTES=67108864

# All unsafe API requests first share one aggregate slot before JSON/multipart
# body parsing, bounding the combined memory of writes, passwords, uploads, and
# AI. Full workspace mutations then use the tighter single-writer-oriented gate.
# REQUEST_BODY_MAX_ACTIVE=8
# REQUEST_BODY_MAX_QUEUED=128
# REQUEST_BODY_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# One verified account (or, before authentication, one client network) may not
# occupy the aggregate body/parser budget. This preserves fairness for many
# different accounts behind a campus/company NAT while still bounding one
# account that reconnects through several source IPs. Queued bodies are
# separately capped so one abusive principal cannot fill the FIFO.
# REQUEST_BODY_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_IP=2
# REQUEST_BODY_MAX_QUEUED_PER_IP=2
# Credential bodies enter a small FIFO before the shared parser pool. This lets
# a legitimate 100-login burst wait safely instead of competing with incomplete
# anonymous bodies or failing with a generic server-unavailable response.
# AUTH_REQUEST_BODY_MAX_ACTIVE=4
# AUTH_REQUEST_BODY_MAX_QUEUED=256
# AUTH_REQUEST_BODY_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# Ordinary JSON bodies must finish arriving within one minute. Multipart and
# system-update packages have bounded longer windows for slower
# uploads. Header receipt is independently capped before routing begins.
# REQUEST_HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# REQUEST_BODY_DEADLINE_MS=60000
# MULTIPART_BODY_DEADLINE_MS=180000
# SYSTEM_UPDATE_BODY_DEADLINE_MS=900000
# MUTATION_MAX_ACTIVE=1
# MUTATION_MAX_QUEUED=64
# MUTATION_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# Safe reads and allocation-heavy API work have independent process-wide
# ceilings. The *_PER_IP names are retained for configuration compatibility;
# they apply to the verified account when a valid session Bearer is present and
# to the normalized client network only for anonymous/invalid credentials.
# STANDARD_WORK_MAX_ACTIVE=32
# STANDARD_WORK_MAX_QUEUED=256
# STANDARD_WORK_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# STANDARD_WORK_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_IP=8
# STANDARD_WORK_MAX_QUEUED_PER_IP=16
# HEAVY_WORK_MAX_ACTIVE=1
# HEAVY_WORK_MAX_QUEUED=32
# HEAVY_WORK_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# HEAVY_WORK_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_IP=1
# HEAVY_WORK_MAX_QUEUED_PER_IP=2

# Password verification is memory-hard. With a 1 GiB runtime budget, twelve
# active checks are aligned to the expanded libuv pool; smaller hosts are
# automatically capped by thread and memory budgets.
# AUTH_PASSWORD_MAX_ACTIVE=12
# AUTH_PASSWORD_MAX_QUEUED=128
# AUTH_PASSWORD_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# Authenticated conditional-response cache (strict per-snapshot LRU + byte cap).
# CONDITIONAL_CACHE_MAX_ENTRIES_PER_STORE=256
# CONDITIONAL_CACHE_MAX_BYTES_PER_STORE=16777216
# CONDITIONAL_CACHE_MAX_ENTRY_BYTES=2097152

# Long-lived collaboration and health channels.
# PHD_ATLAS_REALTIME_MAX_CONNECTIONS=256
# PHD_ATLAS_HEALTH_WS_MAX_CONNECTIONS=512
# PHD_ATLAS_HEALTH_WS_MAX_CONNECTIONS_PER_IP=256

# Downloads and incremental workspace responses use a separate bounded stream
# pool. Queued streams reserve no buffer; each active stream reserves only the
# configured chunk budget. STREAM_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS is reset by successful output
# progress and is deliberately not an absolute transfer deadline.
# STREAM_MAX_ACTIVE=32
# STREAM_MAX_QUEUED=256
# STREAM_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_PRINCIPAL=4
# STREAM_MAX_QUEUED_PER_PRINCIPAL=8
# STREAM_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# STREAM_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# STREAM_BUFFER_RESERVATION_BYTES=524288
# Large workspace streams serialize only their compact authorization/scope
# snapshot and largest-row reservation before committing HTTP 200. Body
# transfers still use STREAM_MAX_ACTIVE concurrently.
# WORKSPACE_STREAM_PREP_MAX_ACTIVE=1
# WORKSPACE_STREAM_PREP_MAX_QUEUED=256
# WORKSPACE_STREAM_PREP_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# External MySQL/PostgreSQL/MSSQL query and transaction deadline. Values are
# clamped to 1–60 seconds so a stalled database cannot hold maintenance forever.
# PHD_ATLAS_EXTERNAL_DB_QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
# First-run setup rejects private/reserved database targets by default. List
# exact operator-approved hostnames or IP literals only when the database is
# intentionally private; comma-separated values are normalized before use.
# DATABASE_PRIVATE_HOST_ALLOWLIST=db.internal.example,10.20.30.40

# Keep one HTTP listener alive while retryable durable-storage startup failures
# recover. Backoff is jittered and capped but attempts are intentionally not
# capped; permanent configuration/authentication/state errors still fail fast.
# STORAGE_STARTUP_RETRY_BASE_MS=250
# STORAGE_STARTUP_RETRY_MAX_MS=8000

# Optional upload/Web Push/browser-journal startup attempts run behind core
# readiness. Upload migration gets a larger per-attempt window and is
# cooperatively aborted before the degraded subsystem is retried in background.
# STARTUP_SUBSYSTEM_ATTEMPT_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# UPLOAD_VAULT_STARTUP_ATTEMPT_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# Cross-process upload migration lock waits are independently bounded so one
# abandoned worker cannot hold a replacement worker in startup forever.
# PHD_ATLAS_UPLOAD_MIGRATION_LOCK_WAIT_MS=20000

# The worker gets at most 20 seconds for cooperative HTTP/background drain,
# followed by a 40-second primary window of referenced, exponential storage-
# shutdown retries. If durability is still resident, low-frequency retries
# continue at no more than one per five seconds until recovery or a real outer
# stop ceiling. The worker exits only after durability is confirmed. Never
# configure an outer stop window below 75s.
# SERVER_SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_TIMEOUT_MS=18000
# PHD_ATLAS_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
# PHD_ATLAS_STORAGE_SHUTDOWN_RETRY_MS=40000

# Durable delivery recovery is deliberately serial and starts after a short
# settle window, so reconnecting clients do not compete with seven background
# database/SMTP jobs immediately after storage becomes ready.
# STARTUP_RECOVERY_CONCURRENCY=1
# STARTUP_RECOVERY_INITIAL_DELAY_MS=2000
# STARTUP_RECOVERY_STAGGER_MS=150

# AI route and provider admission. Response/body limits prevent a slow or
# malformed upstream from retaining unbounded memory.
# AI_MAX_ACTIVE=1
# AI_MAX_QUEUED=32
# AI_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_USER=1
# AI_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_KEY=1
# AI_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=12000
# AI_DRAFT_MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES=262144
# AI_PROVIDER_MAX_ACTIVE=2
# AI_PROVIDER_MAX_QUEUED=64
# AI_PROVIDER_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_OWNER=1
# AI_PROVIDER_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_KEY=1
# AI_PROVIDER_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# AI_PROVIDER_STREAM_MAX_BYTES=8388608
# AI_PROVIDER_JSON_MAX_BYTES=8388608
# AI_PROVIDER_SSE_BUFFER_MAX_BYTES=1048576
# AI_PROVIDER_DRAFT_TIMEOUT_MS=180000
# AI_PROVIDER_COMPLETION_TIMEOUT_MS=180000
# AI_DRAFT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=195000
# AI_KEY_TEST_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=45000
# AI_RESEARCH_START_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# AI_ENRICHMENT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=480000
# AI_DRAFT_HEARTBEAT_MS=20000

# Discover source acquisition stays deliberately narrow because every bounded
# upstream response can still retain several MiB until it is parsed.
# DISCOVER_RESEARCH_CONCURRENCY=1
# DISCOVER_RESEARCH_MAX_BACKLOG=512
# DISCOVER_RESEARCH_TIME_SLICE_MS=900000
# DISCOVER_CRAWL_CONCURRENCY=3
# DISCOVER_GLOBAL_COUNTRY_CONCURRENCY=2
# DISCOVER_GLOBAL_SEARCH_CONCURRENCY=1
# DISCOVER_GLOBAL_DETAIL_CONCURRENCY=2

# Encrypted upload I/O. The active-byte budget must remain at least one maximum
# file plus roughly 512 KiB of streaming workspace.
# PHD_ATLAS_UPLOAD_IO_MAX_CONCURRENT=2
# PHD_ATLAS_UPLOAD_IO_MAX_QUEUED=64
# PHD_ATLAS_UPLOAD_IO_MAX_IN_FLIGHT_BYTES=33554432
# PHD_ATLAS_UPLOAD_IO_MAX_QUEUED_BYTES=67108864
# PHD_ATLAS_UPLOAD_IO_WAIT_MS=20000
# PHD_ATLAS_UPLOAD_MAX_FILE_BYTES=26214400

# Proxy trust setting (the bare server default is false). Docker Compose safely
# supplies one exact hop because port 4317 is bound to host loopback only; the
# supplied systemd unit instead pins same-host Nginx to `loopback`. Override the
# Compose value only with your exact hop count, CIDR, or named subnet. Production
# refuses TRUST_PROXY=true because it trusts arbitrary forwarding hops.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# Pin a specific image tag for reproducible deployments
# PHD_ATLAS_IMAGE=ghcr.io/zhoujasper/phd-atlas:latest

# ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Secrets (auto-generated — override only if migrating an existing install)
# ───────────────────────────────────────────
# JWT_SECRET=
# SETTINGS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# The required first-run PHD_ATLAS_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN is declared at the top of
# this file. JWT/encryption keys may remain blank and are persisted on storage.

# ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Anti-abuse and account security
# ───────────────────────────────────────────

# Recommended for every public registration page. Create a Turnstile widget
# with action "signup". Both values are required to enable the widget.
# TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
# TURNSTILE_REQUIRED=1

# Restrict registration by email domain (comma-separated; allowlist wins when set).
# REGISTRATION_ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS=
# REGISTRATION_BLOCKED_EMAIL_DOMAINS=mailinator.com,guerrillamail.com

# Extra providers where local+tag aliases should count as one signup identity.
# Gmail/Googlemail dot and plus aliases are normalized automatically.
# REGISTRATION_ALIAS_DOMAINS=outlook.com

# Successful-account velocity controls (safe defaults shown).
# REGISTRATION_MAX_PER_IP_DAY=5
# REGISTRATION_MAX_PER_SUBNET_DAY=20
# REGISTRATION_MAX_PER_DOMAIN_DAY=50
# REGISTRATION_MAX_GLOBAL_DAY=500

# Verification-email velocity controls.
# REGISTRATION_EMAIL_MAX_PER_NETWORK_10M=8
# REGISTRATION_EMAIL_MAX_PER_ADDRESS_30M=3
# REGISTRATION_EMAIL_MAX_PER_DOMAIN_HOUR=40
# REGISTRATION_EMAIL_MAX_GLOBAL_HOUR=250

# Failed-login controls are independent per network, account, and pair.
# LOGIN_MAX_FAILURES_PER_NETWORK=30
# LOGIN_MAX_FAILURES_PER_ACCOUNT=10
# LOGIN_MAX_FAILURES_PER_PAIR=5

# Distributed challenge and password-reset controls.
# HUMAN_CHALLENGE_MAX_GLOBAL_HOUR=5000
# PASSWORD_RESET_MAX_PER_NETWORK_15M=10
# PASSWORD_RESET_MAX_PER_ACCOUNT_HOUR=3
# PASSWORD_RESET_MAX_GLOBAL_HOUR=250

# Production checks only the first five SHA-1 characters against the HIBP
# Pwned Passwords range API. It fails open during provider outages by default.
# PWNED_PASSWORDS_CHECK=1
# PWNED_PASSWORDS_FAIL_CLOSED=0

# Expensive authenticated operations.
# DISCOVER_RESEARCH_MAX_PER_USER_HOUR=12
# AI_DRAFT_MAX_PER_USER_HOUR=60
# OUTGOING_EMAIL_MAX_PER_USER_HOUR=30
# BACKUP_MUTATIONS_MAX_PER_USER_HOUR=12

# Scholarly-lead API identity. Discover still requires official university
# pages before a programme or advisor can be saved.
#
# OPENALEX_API_KEY is effectively required for advisor discovery. OpenAlex now
# meters requests against a daily budget and charges beyond it; once that
# budget is spent, every endpoint refuses with "Insufficient budget" until the
# next UTC midnight, and the mailto polite pool no longer raises the ceiling.
# Without a key the scholarly candidate pool is empty, so Discover falls back
# to crawled faculty pages alone and returns noticeably fewer advisors. The run
# reports this as the `scholarly-provider-quota-exhausted` quality warning
# rather than failing outright. Keys: https://openalex.org/pricing
# OPENALEX_API_KEY=
# OPENALEX_MAILTO=
# CROSSREF_MAILTO=
# NOTIFICATION_PUBLISH_MAX_PER_ADMIN_HOUR=30

# Production mail destinations must resolve only to public addresses. If this
# installation intentionally uses private SMTP/IMAP, allow exact normalized
# hostnames (never a wildcard or subnet).
# MAIL_PRIVATE_HOST_ALLOWLIST=mail.internal.example

# Leave unset: production otherwise requires direct TLS or mandatory STARTTLS.
# This emergency compatibility switch can expose mailbox credentials.
# MAIL_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT=1

# ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional: custom CA certificates for private databases/mail servers
# NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/app/storage/certs/private-ca.pem

# Optional: CJK PDF font paths
# PHD_ATLAS_PDF_FONT=
# PHD_ATLAS_PDF_FONT_BOLD=
